Roast.pet Frontier Stack =E2=80=94 2 useful finds 1. HappyOyster 1.0 Acting=
 /
Directing =E2=80=94 TEST NOW

Source: https://docs.qwencloud.com/changelog/models and
https://docs.qwencloud.com/developer-guides/getting-started/world-model/hap=
pyoyster-guide
Release: 2026-09-18.

What changed: QwenCloud launched three HappyOyster real-time world-model
modes. The Roast.pet-relevant modes are Acting and Directing. Acting takes
text + a first-frame image and creates a face-to-face interactive character
that responds in real time with voice, expressions and actions while
maintaining appearance, voice and personality for up to 3 minutes.
Directing accepts text, first-frame or multiple reference images and
streams up to 3 minutes while accepting commands during generation; it
supports pause, rewind, branching, and reference-image subject/prop locking=
.

Capability: This is qualitatively different from the current pre-rendered
talking-pet pipeline. It could make the QR/AR endpoint itself interactive:
scan the physical card, see your own pet, speak to it, and have the pet
roast you back in real time. Directing could also create longer consistent
social clips without separately generating every shot.

I/O: Acting =3D text + first-frame image -> real-time interactive audiovisu=
al
stream. Directing =3D text / first frame / multiple references + live
commands -> streaming audiovisual video up to 3 minutes.
API/install: QwenCloud documentation says API/SDK integration is available;
console is also available. Exact public endpoint details/pricing were not
exposed clearly enough in this scan to quote safely.
Pricing: not verified; benchmark before productization.
Commercial/license: hosted QwenCloud service; commercial terms need
verification for this new world-model SKU. No open weights/self-host route
established.
Privacy: cloud-hosted. Use Roast.pet canonical assets, not original
customer uploads, unless required.
Quality/latency constraints: vendor docs claim real-time interaction and
3-minute consistency, but examples are primarily human/stylized characters.
Animal identity, dog/cat mouth behavior and actual geographic latency are
unproven.
Exact use case: QR card -> interactive pet that responds to the owner
rather than playing one fixed roast video.
Smallest prototype: upload one canonical dog podium image into Acting,
define a sarcastic pet persona and voice, then run a 2-minute conversation.
Test identity drift, animal-mouth plausibility, turn latency, voice
consistency and whether user speech can reliably trigger short roast
responses. Then repeat with five difficult breeds/cats.
Decision: TEST NOW, but as an experimental V2 AR mode rather than blocking
V1.
2. qwen3.8-omni-flash =E2=80=94 TEST as automated media QC

Source: https://docs.qwencloud.com/changelog/models and
https://docs.qwencloud.com/developer-guides/speech/omni-models
Release: 2026-09-18.

What changed: new native omni model with up to 1M context,
text/image/audio/video input, integrated audiovisual understanding,
subtitle generation, tool calling and explicit positioning for video
production/editing workflows. It supports DashScope and OpenAI-compatible
protocols.

Capability relevant to Roast.pet: one cheap evaluator can inspect the
finished roast video as audiovisual media rather than running separate
image-identity, transcript, audio and timing checks. It can potentially
judge whether the pet still matches the reference, whether the spoken joke
matches the script, subtitle correctness, dead air, bad cuts, audio
problems and obvious visual failures before an order is delivered.
Inputs/outputs: pet reference + generated MP4/audio/text -> structured
text/QC verdict. Video-file understanding includes audio; current docs
allow up to 256 MB / 150 seconds for the lightweight Omni-Flash family.
API/install: QwenCloud Chat Completions/Responses, OpenAI-compatible
protocol; companion Qwen-MM-Plugins recommended for agent frameworks.
Pricing: token-based by modality; exact qwen3.8-omni-flash rate was not
sufficiently clear in the retrieved official docs, so measure actual bill.
Commercial/license: hosted API; no self-host weights established for this
exact Omni model.
Privacy: generated media and supplied references leave our infrastructure.
Prefer canonical assets rather than original customer photos.
Latency/quality: streaming required; thinking mode can add latency and
audio output is unavailable in thinking mode, which does not matter for QC.
Animal identity scoring still needs calibration against human labels.
Exact use case: final_render.mp4 + canonical_pet.png + expected_script.json
-> PASS / RETRY_VIDEO / RETRY_AUDIO / FIX_CAPTIONS with reasons and
confidence.
Smallest prototype: score 50 intentionally mixed good/bad Roast.pet outputs
and compare its verdicts to human labels. Only adopt if false-pass rate is
very low.
Decision: TEST.
TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW

   1. HappyOyster Acting: interactive QR pet roast proof-of-concept.
   2. qwen3.8-omni-flash as unified audiovisual QC gate.
   3. Wan 2.7 audio-driven I2V vs H3 Max Lip Sync for deterministic roast
   performances.
   4. Alphaveil/BiRefNet -> EdgeTAM -> ViTMatte browser-local customer
   image preparation.
   5. Multi-reference canonical pet identity + cheap render -> QC ->
   premium escalation.

CHEAPEST meaningful capability improvement: browser-local pet
segmentation/refinement remains effectively $0 marginal inference.

Biggest capability shock this hour: HappyOyster Acting. The product no
longer has to end at =E2=80=9Cscan QR and watch your pet roast you.=E2=80=
=9D A newly
released model class can plausibly turn the personalized pet asset into a
persistent real-time character that the customer actually talks to.

Concrete stack simplification: add a canonical PetAsset + PetPersona
boundary now. V1 renders fixed video from it; experimental V2 passes the
exact same PetAsset/Persona into an InteractiveRenderer such as
HappyOyster. This avoids rebuilding identity/personality logic if
interactive generated media becomes good enough.

No mature unchanged tools were padded into this report.

